---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base title="Jewellness" description="A start-up project from Hridal's Entrepreneurship module: the problem with fitness trackers, the customer research, and the design for a health-tracking bracelet that looks like jewellery.">
  <article class="case">
    <a class="case-back" href="/projects">All projects</a>
    <p class="case-kind">Start-up, Imperial Entrepreneurship module, October 2026 to now</p>
    <h1>Jewellness</h1>
    <p class="case-lede">A start-up idea from my Entrepreneurship module: a bracelet that tracks sleep, heart health and your cycle with every sensor hidden in the clasp, so it looks like jewellery rather than a fitness tracker.</p>

    <figure class="case-viewer">
      <div class="viewer-box app-box" id="viewer">
        <div class="app-poster">
          <p class="app-name">3D model of the design</p>
          <p class="app-sub">Turn it, change the metal and stones, and look at the clasp from the skin side.</p>
          <button class="btn solid" id="load-viewer">Open the 3D model here</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>A concept model I made to work out the design, not a finished product. <a href="/projects/jewellness/viewer.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open it full screen</a></figcaption>
    </figure>

    <dl class="case-facts">
      <div><dt>My role</dt><dd>The idea, customer research, design and the market sizing and financial model</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Research</dt><dd>Interviews with 30+ potential customers</dd></div>
    </dl>

    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>Fitness trackers only help if people actually wear them, and a lot of people stop. Around 42% lose interest within six months, 29% of people in the UK find them unattractive, and almost half are worried about what happens to their data. I noticed the same gap myself, because I wanted something that did what a tracker does without looking like one, and I could not find it.</p>
    <img class="case-slide" src="/projects/jewellness/problem.jpg" alt="Chart of why people stop wearing fitness trackers" loading="lazy" />

    <h2>What I found</h2>
    <p>I spoke to more than 30 people to check whether the problem was real beyond my own experience. Seven in ten were uncomfortable with how a smartwatch or obvious tracker looks, everyone I spoke to said they would rather not pay a subscription, and 62% named sleep tracking as the thing they cared about most.</p>

    <h2>What I designed</h2>
    <p>Those answers shaped the design. The idea is to keep every electronic part inside the clasp, which sits on the underside of the wrist where sensors can stay against the skin, and to use ordinary halo links on top so it wears like a normal tennis bracelet. The design goals that came out of the interviews were a battery that lasts around two weeks, no subscription, a tap-for-help alert for safety, and health data that stays on the wearer's phone. I made the 3D model above to test how that would look and fit together.</p>

    <h2>Checking the numbers</h2>
    <p>To see whether the idea could stand up as a business, I built a market sizing and financial model. I started from the UK wearables market, which my sources put at about US$5.9bn, narrowed it to the roughly US$1.8bn spent on health and fitness wearables by women, and worked out what a 1% share within five years would mean, which came to around US$18m a year. I used it as a sense check on whether the gap was worth pursuing rather than as a forecast.</p>

    <h2>Where it is now</h2>
    <p>I am testing demand with a low-cost pretotype before building anything expensive, because the whole idea depends on people actually wanting to wear it every day.</p>
  </article>
